steps.body:
Two aliases of `string` are the same type, so the checker can't tell an order ID from a customer ID:

```typescript types
type OrderId = string;
type CustomerId = string;
```

A brand fixes that. Intersect the base type with an object type whose property no real string has:

```typescript types
type OrderId = string & { readonly __brand: "OrderId" };
type CustomerId = string & { readonly __brand: "CustomerId" };
```

The two `__brand` types differ, so the two IDs are now different types. The brand exists only for the checker: at run time an `OrderId` is a plain string with no extra property.

Since no string literal has a `__brand`, the way in is a type guard. It checks the string, and when it returns `true`, the string is narrowed to the brand:

```typescript
type OrderId = string & { readonly __brand: "OrderId" };

function isOrderId(s: string): s is OrderId {
  return /^ord_\d{4}$/.test(s);
}

const raw: string = "ord_1001";
console.log(isOrderId(raw) ? `order ${raw}` : "not an order id"); // order ord_1001
```

The regular expression `/^ord_\d{4}$/` matches `ord_` followed by exactly four digits, and `test` returns whether a string matches it.

examples.code:
type OrderId = string;
type CustomerId = string;

const ORDERS = new Map<OrderId, string>([
  ["ord_1001", "2 mugs"],
  ["ord_1002", "1 teapot"],
]);

